WEBVTT

00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:04.240
The style now called suspiciously artificial was often

00:00:04.240 --> 00:00:08.030
taught to students as good professional writing years

00:00:08.030 --> 00:00:09.451
before generative AI.

00:00:15.450 --> 00:00:19.302
Read an old institutional email or academic abstract.

00:00:19.302 --> 00:00:23.154
You may find the immaculate, depersonalized language we

00:00:23.154 --> 00:00:25.561
now accuse machines of inventing.

00:00:31.450 --> 00:00:34.099
Universities demand standard structure, impeccable

00:00:34.099 --> 00:00:37.807
polish and phrases approved by gatekeepers. Writers

00:00:37.807 --> 00:00:40.985
learn to hide any particular voice.

00:00:46.450 --> 00:00:49.941
When software produces the very language institutions

00:00:49.941 --> 00:00:53.931
rewarded, people suddenly decide that the sound itself

00:00:53.931 --> 00:00:54.929
is incriminating.

00:01:01.450 --> 00:01:05.550
A sentence pattern is not evidence of who conceived an

00:01:05.550 --> 00:01:08.419
idea, performed an experiment or understood a

00:01:08.419 --> 00:01:08.829
conclusion.

00:01:16.450 --> 00:01:20.508
Institutions rewarded an impersonal register and now

00:01:20.508 --> 00:01:24.565
treat its imitation as intellectual laziness. That

00:01:24.565 --> 00:01:26.304
contradiction deserves examination.

00:01:31.450 --> 00:01:35.910
What does this author know? Are the sources real? Can

00:01:35.910 --> 00:01:38.587
the argument survive direct examination? Those

00:01:38.587 --> 00:01:39.925
questions require judgment.

00:01:46.450 --> 00:01:50.197
The argument is about who set linguistic rules and who

00:01:50.197 --> 00:01:52.070
gets punished for obeying them.
